  @@ -1200,6 +1208,45 @@ You can add the ``--analyze`` option to run ``ANALYZE`` against the table after
  +.. _cli_inserting_data_json_key:
  +
  +Inserting records held under a top-level key
  +--------------------------------------------
  +
  +Some JSON exports wrap their records in an envelope object, like this ``alaska.json`` file:
  +
  +.. code-block:: json
  +
  +    {
  +        "Version": "5.5.52.6",
  +        "List": [
  +            {"Description": "Nonpartisan", "Id": 1, "ExternalId": ""},
  +            {"Description": "Undeclared", "Id": 2, "ExternalId": ""}
  +        ]
  +    }
  +
  +By default a top-level JSON object is inserted as a single row, so this file would become one row with a ``Version`` column and a ``List`` column holding the records as JSON text.
  +
  +Use ``--key`` to read the records from a named top-level key instead:
  +
  +.. code-block:: bash
  +
  +    sqlite-utils insert data.db records alaska.json --key List
  +
  +This creates a ``records`` table with two rows and the columns ``Description``, ``Id`` and ``ExternalId``. The other top-level keys, here ``Version``, are ignored.
  +
  +If the value of that key is a list, each item in the list is inserted as a row. If the value is an object, that object is inserted as a single row. An empty list inserts no rows. The command exits with an error if the key does not exist, if its value is a string, number, boolean or null, or if the top-level JSON value is not an object.
  +
  +Use ``--auto-key`` to have ``sqlite-utils`` find the key for you:
  +
  +.. code-block:: bash
  +
  +    sqlite-utils insert data.db records alaska.json --auto-key
  +
  +``--auto-key`` looks for the one top-level key whose value is a non-empty list of objects - other keys can sit beside it. It exits with an error that lists the candidate keys if more than one key qualifies, and with an error if none do. An empty list is never a candidate. If the top-level JSON value is already a list, ``--auto-key`` imports it as normal.
  +
  +Both options work with ``sqlite-utils insert``, ``sqlite-utils upsert``, ``sqlite-utils bulk`` and ``sqlite-utils memory``. Options such as ``--flatten``, ``--convert`` and ``--pk`` apply to each extracted record. ``--key`` and ``--auto-key`` cannot be used together, and they only apply to JSON input, so they cannot be combined with ``--csv``, ``--tsv``, ``--nl``, ``--lines`` or ``--text``.
  +
